  overview: "Intersection syndrome is a painful overuse condition of the dorsoradial distal forearm. It presents with pain and swelling where the muscle bellies of abductor pollicis longus (APL) and extensor pollicis brevis (EPB), the first dorsal compartment muscles, cross over the radial wrist extensors, extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis (ECRL and ECRB), which lie in the second dorsal compartment [r1,r2]. In climbers the crossing point was about 5 cm proximal to Lister's tubercle, with swelling and crepitus [r3], and an MRI of suspected proximal intersection syndrome needs to include the area roughly 4 to 8 cm proximal to Lister's tubercle [r4]. It is a classic problem of rowers (oarsman's wrist) and is also described in racquet sports, baseball, cycling, hockey, golf, ice hockey, skiing, softball and climbing [r3].\n\nThe underlying pathology is not settled. A surgical series of 13 patients concluded that the basic abnormality is stenosing tenosynovitis of the sheath of the common radial wrist extensors [r1]. An elite-rowing group regard it as a true tendinitis of the second compartment caused by fascial compression from hypertrophied first-compartment muscles [r5]. Others describe friction or rubbing at the crossing point with peritenosynovitis [r2,r6], and a dynamic ultrasound case showed EPB muscle belly hypertrophy and oedema with radial displacement of the second-compartment tendons during the aggravating movement [r7]. These are not competing certainties: most of the literature is case reports and small series, and the authors of the rowing series describe continuing conjecture over its pathology and best management [r5].\n\nManagement evidence is correspondingly weak. A review of the treatment literature describes rest, a thumb spica splint, analgesics and oral NSAIDs, followed after 2–3 weeks by progressive stretching and strengthening, with corticosteroid injection adjacent to the site if symptoms persist and surgery for refractory cases [r6]. Family-medicine guidance is similar and adds physical or occupational therapy and ultrasound-guided injection for non-responders [r8]. No randomised trial was retrieved. It is commonly confused with de Quervain's tenosynovitis, which sits at the radial styloid [r6,r9].",

