    summary: "There is usually no single injury moment. Repeated or sustained wrist extension against a gripped handle, with the forearm pronated and the wrist in ulnar deviation, is proposed to make the APL and EPB muscle bellies rub over or compress the ECRL and ECRB tendons at the crossing point, 4–8 cm proximal to Lister's tubercle [r4,r9,r15]. In a cadaver model, pronation with ulnar deviation (without flexion or dorsiflexion) produced the strongest pressure on the distal radius from APL and EPB, and the authors concluded that this position increases the risk of intersection syndrome [r15]. Whether the resulting problem is a friction tenosynovitis of the second-compartment tendons [r1,r5] or a peritendinous inflammation at the crossing is debated.",
